-- README.menu for HackedBox 0.9.x --

HackedBox Menu Configuration

HackedBox menus are plain text files that define the commands, submenus,
styles, workspaces, and other functions available from the root menu.

A menu file may be stored anywhere the user has read access to. The location
of the active menu is specified by the session.menuFile resource.

The default menu installed with HackedBox is located under:

/usr/share/hackedbox/menu

User-defined menus are normally kept in:

~/.hackedbox/
Menu Syntax

A menu entry normally consists of up to three fields:

[tag] (label) {command}

Some entries may also have an optional icon field:

[tag] (label) {command} </path/to/icon>

The fields used depend on the tag. Some tags use only a label, some use the
label as a filename or directory, and some use the command field for
additional data.

Icons are enclosed in '<' and '>' and may be used by menu entries that
support icons.

For example:

[exec] (Terminal) {xterm} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/apps/terminal.svg>

[submenu] (Configuration) </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
Menu Tags

[begin] (label)

Starts the root menu. The label is used as the menu title.

Example:

    [begin] (HackedBox)

A menu file must contain a [begin] entry. If HackedBox cannot find
a valid [begin] entry, the default menu is used.

[end]

Ends the current menu or submenu.

Example:

    [end]

[exec] (label) {command} </path/to/icon>

Adds an entry that executes the specified command when selected.

Normal commands can be used:

    [exec] (Terminal) {xterm}
    [exec] (File Manager) {fileman}
    [exec] (Web Browser) {firefox}

HackedBox also provides special handling for the built-in RunBox.

The built-in RunBox launcher is opened with:

    [exec] (Run) {@launcher@}

The command @launcher@ is recognized by HackedBox and opens the
built-in RunBox instead of being passed to the shell.

Special menu variables may also be used in [exec] commands:

    [exec] (Menu File) {cudatext $menu}
    [exec] (RC File) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/hackedbox.rc}
    [exec] (Keys File) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/keys}
    [exec] (Style File) {cudatext $style}

[exec] entries may optionally specify an icon after the command field.

Example:

    [exec] (Cudatext) {cudatext} </usr/src/cudatext/cudatext-512.png>

The icon pathname is enclosed in '<' and '>'.

The icon is optional. An [exec] entry without an icon remains valid:

    [exec] (Terminal) {xterm}

The icon pathname may be absolute or may refer to any pathname that
can be opened by the HackedBox process.

HackedBox scales the icon proportionally to fit the menu's icon area.

[exit] (label)

Adds an entry that exits HackedBox.

Example:

    [exit] (Exit HackedBox)

Open windows are reparented to the root window before HackedBox exits.

[include] (filename)

Includes another menu file at the current location.

The filename must be enclosed in parentheses. Curly braces {}
are not used for [include].

The filename may be an absolute path or may begin with ~/ to refer
to the user's home directory. Paths are not resolved relative to
the directory containing the current menu file.

Example:

    [include] (~/.hackedbox/menus/applications)

The included file is parsed as part of the current menu at the
location of the [include] entry.

If the filename is missing, cannot be opened, or is not a regular
file, an error is reported and the include is skipped.

[nop] (label)

Adds a non-operational menu item.

A [nop] entry can be used as a separator or section heading.

The label is optional.

Examples:

    [nop]
    [nop] (Applications)

[style] (label) {filename}

Adds an entry that loads a style file.

The filename may be an absolute path or may begin with ~/.

Example:

    [style] (Default) {/usr/share/hackedbox/styles/Default}

Selecting the entry loads that style.

[submenu] (label) {title} <icon>

Creates a submenu.

The label is the name displayed in the parent menu.

The optional command field specifies the submenu title. If no title
is supplied, the label is used as the submenu title.

An optional icon may be specified after the label or title.

Example:

    [submenu] (Edit Config Files) </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
        [exec] (menu) {cudatext $menu} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
        [exec] (rc) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/hackedbox.rc} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/edit.svg>
        [exec] (keys) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/keys} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/devices/input-keyboard.svg>
        [exec] (Style) {cudatext $style} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
    [end]

A separate submenu title may be specified with the command field:

    [submenu] (Configuration) {HackedBox Configuration}
        [exec] (Menu File) {cudatext $menu}
    [end]

Submenus may contain any other supported menu entries, including
additional submenus.

[config] (label)

Adds the HackedBox configuration menu.

Example:

    [config] (Configuration)

The configuration menu provides access to options stored in the
HackedBox configuration.

[reconfig] (label)

Reloads the current HackedBox configuration.

Example:

    [reconfig] (Reconfigure)

This reloads the menu and style configuration without requiring
HackedBox to be completely restarted.

[restart] (label) {command}

Restarts HackedBox.

If a command is supplied, HackedBox restarts using that command.

Examples:

    [restart] (Restart HackedBox)
    [restart] (Restart Other WM) {otherwm}

If no command is supplied, HackedBox restarts itself.

[workspaces] (label)

Adds the HackedBox workspace menu to the current menu.

Example:

    [workspaces] (Workspaces)

[clock]

Adds the HackedBox clock item to the current menu.

The clock format is controlled by the current menu/style configuration.

Example:

    [clock]

[date]

Adds the HackedBox date item to the current menu.

The date format is controlled by the current menu/style configuration.

Example:

    [date]

[stylesdir] (directory)

Scans a directory for style files and adds the discovered files to
the current menu.

The directory may be an absolute path or may begin with ~/.

Example:

    [submenu] (Styles) {Choose a style...}
        [nop] ( *** User Styles *** )
        [stylesdir] (~/.hackedbox/styles)
    [end]

HackedBox scans the directory when the menu is parsed. Regular files
found in the directory are added as style entries automatically.

The files are sorted alphabetically before they are added to the menu.

Files ending in '~' are ignored.

Each discovered filename becomes the menu label and the complete
pathname becomes the style file loaded when that entry is selected.

For example, if the directory contains:

    ~/.hackedbox/styles/Default
    ~/.hackedbox/styles/BlueNight
    ~/.hackedbox/styles/Dark

The resulting menu contains entries equivalent to:

    [style] (BlueNight) {~/.hackedbox/styles/BlueNight}
    [style] (Dark) {~/.hackedbox/styles/Dark}
    [style] (Default) {~/.hackedbox/styles/Default}

This makes [stylesdir] useful for user-managed style collections.

Adding or removing a style file changes the generated style menu the
next time the menu is parsed or HackedBox is reconfigured.

[stylesmenu] (label) {directory}

Creates a submenu populated from a style directory.

The label becomes the submenu name. The directory is supplied in
the command field.

The directory may be an absolute path or may begin with ~/.

Example:

    [stylesmenu] (User Styles) {~/.hackedbox/styles}

HackedBox scans the directory and adds regular files as style entries.

The files are sorted alphabetically before they are added to the menu.

Files ending in '~' are ignored.

Unlike [stylesdir], [stylesmenu] creates a separate submenu for the
discovered styles.
Special Variables

HackedBox provides variables that can be used inside command fields.

$menu

Expands to the path of the currently active menu file.

The value comes from HackedBox's current menu configuration,
specifically the session.menuFile resource.

Example:

    [exec] (Menu File) {cudatext $menu}

This allows a menu entry to open the menu currently being used by
HackedBox without hard-coding its pathname.

$style

Expands to the path of the currently active style file.

The value comes from HackedBox's current style configuration.

Example:

    [exec] (Style File) {cudatext $style}

This allows a menu entry to open the style currently being used by
HackedBox without hard-coding its pathname.

@launcher@

Opens the built-in HackedBox RunBox launcher.

Example:

    [exec] (Run) {@launcher@}

@launcher@ is a HackedBox menu command and is not executed as an
external program.
Comments

Comments may be placed on any line by making '#' the first character.

Example:

# Applications
[exec] (Terminal) {xterm}

Comments must begin with '#' at the start of the line.

Escaping

The menu parser uses brackets, parentheses, braces, and angle brackets
to identify the different fields.

Characters that conflict with the menu syntax may be escaped with a
backslash where supported by the parser.

For example:

[exec] (My \(Terminal\)) {xterm}

A literal backslash is written as:

\\

Avoid unnecessary escaping in normal commands.

Menu File Location

The active menu file is selected using the session.menuFile resource.

Example:

session.menuFile: ~/.hackedbox/menu

An absolute path may also be used:

session.menuFile: /home/user/.hackedbox/menu

The menu file can therefore be changed without modifying the HackedBox
executable.

User configuration files are normally stored in:

~/.hackedbox/

System-wide HackedBox data is normally installed under:

/usr/share/hackedbox/

Do not assume that the system-installed menu is the active menu. A user
may replace it with a custom menu simply by changing session.menuFile.

Creating a Basic Menu

A minimal working menu consists of a [begin] and [end] entry:

[begin] (HackedBox)

    [exec] (Terminal) {xterm}
    [exit] (Exit HackedBox)

[end]

A more useful menu might look like:

[begin] (HackedBox)

    [exec] (Run) {@launcher@}

    [nop]

    [exec] (Terminal) {xterm}
    [exec] (File Manager) {fileman}
    [exec] (Web Browser) {firefox}

    [submenu] (Configuration) {HackedBox Configuration}
        [exec] (Menu File) {cudatext $menu}
        [exec] (RC File) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/hackedbox.rc}
        [exec] (Keys File) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/keys}
        [exec] (Style File) {cudatext $style}
    [end]

    [submenu] (Styles) {Choose a style...}
        [nop] ( *** User Styles *** )
        [stylesdir] (~/.hackedbox/styles)
    [end]

    [nop]

    [workspaces] (Workspaces)

    [clock]
    [date]

    [reconfig] (Reconfigure)
    [restart] (Restart HackedBox)
    [exit] (Exit HackedBox)

[end]
Styles

Styles can be added individually using the [style] tag.

Example:

[submenu] (Styles) {HackedBox Styles}
    [style] (Default) {/usr/share/hackedbox/styles/Default}
    [style] (Default) {~/.hackedbox/styles/Default}
[end]

For directories containing multiple user styles, [stylesdir] can be used
instead:

[submenu] (Styles) {Choose a style...}
    [nop] ( *** User Styles *** )
    [stylesdir] (~/.hackedbox/styles)
[end]

[style] creates one explicitly named style entry.

[stylesdir] scans a directory and creates the style entries automatically.

[stylesmenu] creates a submenu and populates it from a style directory.

Style configuration is documented separately in README.style.

Default Entries:

[submenu] (Styles) {Choose a style...}
    [nop] ( *** User Styles *** )
    [stylesdir] (~/.hackedbox/styles)
    [nop] ( *** System Styles *** )
    [stylesdir] (/usr/share/hackedbox/styles)
[end]
Reconfiguring After Changes

The [reconfig] menu entry can be used while developing or modifying a
menu or style.

Example:

[reconfig] (Reconfigure)

Selecting this entry causes HackedBox to reload its current configuration.

This is useful when testing menu and style changes without completely
restarting the window manager.

Menu Includes

Large menus can be split into multiple files using [include].

For example, the main menu may contain:

[begin] (HackedBox)

    [include] (~/.hackedbox/menus/applications)
    [include] (~/.hackedbox/menus/system)

    [exit] (Exit HackedBox)

[end]

The included files are parsed at the location of the [include] entry.

The filename is placed in parentheses. The {command} syntax used by
[exec] is not used by [include].

Included paths may be absolute or may begin with ~/.

Paths are not resolved relative to the directory containing the current
menu file.

Menu File and Style File Editing

A convenient configuration submenu can use HackedBox's special variables:

[submenu] (Edit Config Files) </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
    [exec] (menu) {cudatext $menu} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
    [exec] (rc) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/hackedbox.rc} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/edit.svg>
    [exec] (keys) {cudatext ~/.hackedbox/keys} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/devices/input-keyboard.svg>
    [exec] (Style) {cudatext $style} </usr/share/icons/Papirus-Dark/24x24/actions/configure.svg>
[end]

$menu refers to the path of the currently active menu as maintained by
HackedBox's menu configuration.

$style refers to the path of the currently active style as maintained by
HackedBox's style configuration.

These variables allow configuration entries to follow the active menu
and style without hard-coded paths.

Notes on User Configuration

HackedBox maintains configuration information in:

~/.hackedbox/

The menu file is selected through:

session.menuFile

Other configuration files, including key bindings and the main HackedBox
configuration, are stored separately.

A menu is independent of the HackedBox executable and can be created,
modified, or replaced without rebuilding HackedBox.

After modifying a menu, use [reconfig] to reload it.

Supported Menu Tags Summary
[begin]       Start a menu
[end]          End a menu or submenu
[exec]         Execute a command
[exit]         Exit HackedBox
[include]      Include another menu file
[nop]          Non-operational menu item
[style]        Load a specific style
[stylesdir]    Generate style entries from a directory
[stylesmenu]   Create a submenu from a style directory
[submenu]      Create a submenu
[config]       Add the configuration menu
[reconfig]     Reload configuration
[restart]      Restart HackedBox
[workspaces]   Add the workspace menu
[clock]        Add the clock Line
[date]         Add the date Line